@page "/docs/get-started/existing-app"

@* izzy init (catalog/init, Setup.cs) and what's already in an app. Each kind of app was checked against
   Microsoft's .NET 10 templates (empty and full Blazor Web App, Auto, WebAssembly, MVC, Razor Pages): it builds
   after init. The Bootstrap layering was checked in Chromium against Bootstrap 5.3 and the template's app.css;
   the EditForm case against a .NET 10 app. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="existing-app">
    <Lead>
        <Code>izzy init</Code> sets up IzzyBlazor in an app you have: a Blazor Web App, or an MVC or Razor Pages app. Your
        pages, forms and components keep working beside it, and you use IzzyBlazor page by page.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Set it up</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Install the CLI, then run init in the project's folder, or the solution's for an app with a <Code>.Client</Code> project:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@Init</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Init adds the package and the lines below, then builds the app. To see the changes before anything is written,
            run <Code>izzy init --dry-run --diff</Code>. Running it again changes nothing.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Changes" Caption="What izzy init changes" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="App" Width="Length.Rem(11)">@context!.App</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What izzy init does">@context!.Change</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Init refuses a standalone Blazor WebAssembly app, a Blazor Server app with a <Code>_Host.cshtml</Code>, and an
            app below .NET 10, and says why. Its options:
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Options" Caption="izzy init options" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(15)"><code>@context!.App</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Change</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            To make each change yourself instead, see <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/by-hand">Set up by hand</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">MVC and Razor Pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Init adds a Blazor root beside your views: <Code>Components/App.razor</Code>, a router and a layout. New pages
            go in <Code>Components/Pages</Code> as Razor components with <Code>@@page</Code>, and use that layout. Your views
            and Razor Pages keep <Code>_Layout.cshtml</Code> and Bootstrap, so neither page's CSS meets the other's.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A component and a Razor Page at the same address don't both answer: the component takes it, and the Razor Page
            stops showing. <Code>izzy add legal</Code> warns when <Code>Pages/Privacy.cshtml</Code> is there; give the new
            page another address with <Code>--route</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Keep Bootstrap</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's CSS sits in cascade layers, so any CSS of yours outside a layer wins over it. That makes IzzyBlazor
            easy to adjust. It also means Bootstrap, and rules like the template's <Code>a { color: … }</Code>, override
            IzzyBlazor's components: button links turn blue on blue and headings change size.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            When <Code>App.razor</Code> links Bootstrap, init puts it in a layer of its own, declared before IzzyBlazor's: it
            removes the <Code>&lt;link&gt;</Code>, imports Bootstrap at the top of <Code>app.css</Code>, and links
            <Code>app.css</Code> before IzzyBlazor's stylesheet. Your stylesheet and <Code>App.razor</Code> then look like this:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="css" Title="wwwroot/app.css">@AppCss</Code>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/App.razor" HighlightLines="1-2">@AppHead</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The first layer declared loses to the ones after it, so IzzyBlazor's components win over Bootstrap. Bootstrap's
            classes keep styling your existing markup. Your rules outside a layer still win over both, so init names the
            ones in <Code>app.css</Code> that style bare elements, such as <Code>a</Code> or <Code>html, body</Code>. Move
            them into <Code>@@layer bootstrap { }</Code>, as above, to keep them for your pages. <Code>--keep-css</Code>
            leaves Bootstrap where it was.
        </TextBlock>
        <Alert Status="Status.Info" Title="Your pages get IzzyBlazor's page defaults">
            IzzyBlazor sets the page's font, text color and background, and its focus ring. Once Bootstrap is in a layer, those
            apply to your existing pages too. To keep your own, set them outside a layer, in <Code>app.css</Code>.
        </Alert>

        <Heading Level="2">Name clashes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's components have plain names, such as <Code>Button</Code>, <Code>Card</Code> and <Code>NavMenu</Code>.
            When a component of yours has the same name, the build fails with
            <Code>RZ9985: Multiple components use the tag 'NavMenu'</Code>. Init writes the full name of yours where you use
            it, as for the full template's own <Code>NavMenu</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">&lt;MyApp.Components.Layout.NavMenu /&gt;</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A type with the same name, such as your own <Code>Status</Code> enum, gives an ambiguous reference instead
            (<Code>CS0104</Code>). Init can't see those until the app builds, so it reports them after its build. Qualify one
            (<Code>IzzyBlazor.Status.Success</Code>) or add an alias to <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@@using Status = IzzyBlazor.Status</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">One folder at a time</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>_Imports.razor</Code> applies to its folder and the folders under it. To bring IzzyBlazor in gradually, move
            <Code>@@using IzzyBlazor</Code> from the one in <Code>Components</Code> to a folder's own
            <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>. Only the pages in that folder see its components.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>App.razor</Code> and any layout built with IzzyBlazor need the <Code>@@using</Code> too, at the top of the
            file. Without it, <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme /&gt;</Code> is read as an unknown HTML element, the build only warns, and
            the theme never renders.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text">@Folders</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A page can use a new layout while the rest keep yours. Build one with
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor>, and set it on the page with <Code>@@layout</Code>, or
            as a folder's default layout in its <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Forms you already have</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's fields are Blazor input components, so they work inside your existing <Code>EditForm</Code>, beside
            Blazor's own inputs, with the validator you have:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@EditFormCode</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            They show their own label, hint and errors. For IzzyBlazor's layout and columns, switch the
            <Code>EditForm</Code> to IzzyBlazor's <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>. It has the same
            <Code>Model</Code>, <Code>FormName</Code> and submit events, and adds the DataAnnotations validator itself.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/cli/overview">The izzy CLI</Anchor>: add blocks and pages to the app as code you own.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: every component, with what it does in each render mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/izzy-theme">IzzyTheme</Anchor>: match your brand color and fonts.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string App, string Change);

    private const string Init = """
        dotnet tool install -g IzzyBlazor.Cli --prerelease
        cd MyApp
        izzy init
        """;

    private static readonly Row[] Changes =
    [
        new("Blazor Web App", "Adds the IzzyBlazor package, the stylesheet and IzzyTheme in App.razor, @using IzzyBlazor in _Imports.razor, AddIzzyBlazor() in Program.cs, and a ToastRegion in the layout the router uses."),
        new("With .Client", "The same in the server project. The .Client project gets IzzyBlazor.Core, @using IzzyBlazor and AddIzzyBlazorCore()."),
        new("MVC, Razor Pages", "Adds the IzzyBlazor package and a Blazor root under Components/ (App, Routes, _Imports and a layout), with AddRazorComponents(), UseAntiforgery() and MapRazorComponents<App>() in Program.cs."),
    ];

    private static readonly Row[] Options =
    [
        new("--preset, --brand", "The theme, as in the starter: a preset (Standard, Compact, Soft, Editorial) and a brand color."),
        new("--interactivity Server", "For MVC and Razor Pages: lets a page run on the server over a circuit. Without it every new page is static."),
        new("--keep-css", "Leaves Bootstrap where App.razor links it."),
        new("--mcp", "Connects AI assistants to IzzyBlazor's docs server. A config of yours is kept."),
        new("--no-build", "Skips the build at the end."),
    ];

    private const string AppCss = """
        /* First: puts Bootstrap in a layer that IzzyBlazor's layers come after. */
        @import url("lib/bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css") layer(bootstrap);

        /* Rules for bare elements, in the same layer, so they don't reach IzzyBlazor's components. */
        @layer bootstrap {
            a, .btn-link { color: #006bb7; }
        }

        /* Everything else as before: outside a layer, these still win. */
        .content { padding-top: 1.1rem; }
        """;

    private const string AppHead = """
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/IzzyBlazor.Core/izzyblazor.css"]" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["MyApp.styles.css"]" />
        <IzzyTheme />
        """;

    private const string Folders = """
        Components/
          _Imports.razor           your usings, as before
          App.razor                @using IzzyBlazor at the top, for IzzyTheme
          Pages/
            Orders.razor           untouched
            Settings/
              _Imports.razor       @using IzzyBlazor
              Profile.razor        uses IzzyBlazor
        """;

    private const string EditFormCode = """
        <EditForm Model="Model" FormName="profile" OnValidSubmit="Save">
            <DataAnnotationsValidator />
            <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Email" />
            <InputText @bind-Value="Model!.Nickname" class="form-control" />
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save</button>
        </EditForm>
        """;
}
